  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/panaqolus_albomaculatus.htm",
      "html": "<ul><li>Common names: LDA031, Orange Spot Pleco</li><li>IUCN Red List: Least Concern, due to its wide range and because the threats are localised, but the assessment needs updating (IUCN 2016).</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/panaqolus-albomaculatus-lda-31-venezuela/",
      "html": "<p>The \"typical\" LDA 31 come from Peru and usually have rather whitish spots. Fish from Venezuela, with very bright orange spots, have also been identified as LDA 31.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/panaqolus_albomaculatus.htm",
      "html": "<p>South America: Ecuador: tributary of the Rio Suno. Peru: headwaters of the Napo, Marañon and Ucayali rivers.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/panaqolus-albomaculatus-lda-31-venezuela/",
      "html": "<p>These fish are wood-eaters, as can easily be seen from their spoon-like teeth. (Hypancistrus, by contrast, have pointed teeth and are meat-eaters.)</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/panaqolus_albomaculatus.htm",
      "html": "<p>All Panaque and also the Panaqolus genera are wood eaters, so wood is needed in an aquarium set-up. Also feed vegetable foods such as cucumber, courgette (zucchini) and sinking tablets.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/panaqolus_albomaculatus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Peaceful in the aquarium but not suitable for planted tanks as they are plant eaters.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/panaqolus_albomaculatus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Males have long interopercular spines and their body is adorned with hair-like spines.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/panaqolus-albomaculatus-lda-31-venezuela/",
      "html": "<p>A new L-number, L501, was published in Datz 3/2018. It is a Hypancistrus from Venezuela closely related to Hypancistrus inspector/L201/L339, differing from its known relatives by almost unspotted fins and rather pinkish/orange spots instead of white ones.</p><p>Panaqolus albomaculatus/LDA 31 from Venezuela also have very bright orange spots. The fish examined were 9–12 cm long, almost fully grown. LDA 31 has long been known to be a highly variable species (see Wels Atlas Vol. 2).</p><p>A pair was photographed and examined more closely. They proved without doubt to be wood-eaters, so these fish from Venezuela are simply very pretty LDA 31.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/panaqolus_albomaculatus.htm",
      "html": "<p>The difference between Panaqolus and Panaque is that the latter has an oval-shaped mouth and jaws with very large spatulate teeth with a single cusp, arranged in a V form, while Panaqolus have a small number of spatulate teeth and are also a smaller genus. P. albomaculatus individuals from different localities can differ in the size and colour of the spots. Specimens likely from Ecuador differ from those from Peru in colour pattern.</p><p>Etymology: albomaculatus: albo-, from albus (L.), white; maculatus (L.), spotted, referring to small, rounded, light-coloured spots on head, body and fins.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/panaqolus_albomaculatus.htm",
      "html": "<p>All L-Numbers Datz Special. Habitat, Care &amp; Diet. Seidel, I. 2008. Back to Nature guide to L-catfishes, Ettlingen, Germany 208 p. The ETYFish Project Fish Name Etymology Database © Christopher Scharpf. Velasquez, M., Chocano, L. &amp; Arguello, P. 2016. Panaqolus albomaculatus. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2016.</p>"
    }
  ],
